## Diagnosis

The failure is a genuine production defect, exactly as the architecture and `CLAUDE.md` describe. The one and only production file is `services/web/server.js`, which serves a single hard-coded HTML document for every request. Its heading element reads:

```
<h1>Welcome</h1>
```

`tests/integration/home.spec.ts` asserts that the home page's heading is the store's name:

```
expect(await page.locator('h1').textContent()).toBe('Corner Shop');
```

So the page's served markup says `Welcome` where the store name belongs. `page.locator('h1').textContent()` returns the element's text content verbatim, and `toBe` is exact string equality — no trimming, no normalisation, no retry. The received value `"Welcome"` therefore comes directly from the literal in `server.js`. No harness, config, port, or routing problem is involved: Playwright's `webServer` block in `tests/playwright.config.ts` boots `node ../services/web/server.js` on a path-derived port and `page.goto('/')` reaches it successfully (the sibling link test passes against the same response), which proves delivery works and only the heading's content is wrong.

The contract the spec fixes is: **the home page's single `h1` is the store's name, the exact string `Corner Shop`, and the page keeps offering exactly one anchor pointing at `/hours`.**

## The change

### Part 1 — Correct the heading text (serves the failing test)

**File:** `services/web/server.js` — the `page` template literal, the `<h1>` line.

Replace the heading's text node so the element reads the store name instead of a greeting: the `h1` must contain `Corner Shop` and nothing else. The document already declares the same name in `<title>Corner Shop</title>`, which confirms the intended name and the intended spelling/casing — match it character for character: capital `C`, capital `S`, a single ASCII space between the two words, no trailing punctuation, no `&nbsp;` or other entity, and no HTML markup nested inside the element.

Two mechanical requirements follow from how the assertion reads the DOM, and both must be respected:

- **No incidental whitespace inside the element.** `textContent()` is not trimmed before comparison, so the opening tag, the text, and the closing tag must sit on one line as `<h1>Corner Shop</h1>`. Do not reformat the heading across multiple lines, and do not indent the text onto its own line — either would make `textContent()` return a string with leading/trailing newlines and spaces and the exact-equality assertion would still fail with a confusingly similar-looking value.
- **Keep exactly one `h1` in the document.** The test uses an unqualified `page.locator('h1')`; Playwright's strict mode makes a locator that resolves to more than one element throw on `textContent()`. Do not add a second heading, a visually hidden duplicate, or a wrapper heading anywhere in the served markup.

Prefer the smallest possible edit — changing the text inside the existing `<h1>` — over restructuring the template. If you find it clearer to introduce a single named constant for the store name (e.g. one `const storeName = 'Corner Shop'` interpolated into both `<title>` and `<h1>`), that is acceptable and arguably better, since it removes the chance of the two drifting apart again; but it is optional, and if you do it the rendered bytes must be identical to the hand-written form above, with the interpolation producing no extra whitespace.

### Part 2 — Preserve the opening-hours link (serves the passing sibling test in the same file)

**File:** `services/web/server.js` — the `<a href="/hours">Opening hours</a>` line.

`home.spec.ts > home page > links to the opening hours` asserts `page.locator('a').getAttribute('href')` is `/hours`, and it is currently green. Leave that anchor exactly as it is: one single anchor element in the whole document, with `href` spelled as the root-relative `/hours` (not `hours`, not an absolute URL, not with a query string or trailing slash). This test also relies on strict-mode single resolution, so do not add any further `<a>` elements — no navigation bar, no footer links, no "back" link — while fixing the heading. This part of the plan is a do-not-break constraint rather than new work.

### Part 3 — Refresh the now-stale source comments (serves no test; correctness of the file as left behind)

**File:** `services/web/server.js` — the three-line comment at the top of the file.

